\DOC genvar_index

\TYPE {genvar_index : term list -> int}

\SYNOPSIS
Returns a starting number for fresh variables named like those of genvars.

\DESCRIBE
The call {genvar_index tms} returns the least number {n >= 1} such that no
variable named {_vm} for any number {m >= n} occurs, free or bound, in any of
the terms {tms} (only names that are {_v} followed by the decimal
representation of a number, as made by {string_of_int}, are considered). So
the variables {mk_genvar(n,ty)}, {mk_genvar(n + 1,ty)}, ... are fresh for all
the terms {tms}, whatever the types. It is 1 if there are no such variables.

\FAILURE
Fails only if a variable named by the largest representable integer occurs.

\EXAMPLE
{
  # genvar_index [`\x _v3:num. _v1 + x`; `_v10 /\ _v007`];;
  val it : int = 11
}

\USES
Making fresh variables one at a time in a derived rule, as in
{let n = genvar_index tms in ... mk_genvar(n,ty1) ... mk_genvar(n + 1,ty2)},
without any global state. Where all the variables can be made at once,
{genvars} is simpler.

\SEEALSO
genvar, genvar_index_goal, genvars, mk_genvar.

\ENDDOC
